```

Statement: "A connector like this reads the committed outbox inserts from PostgreSQL''s write-ahead log and publishes them, so the api still writes the outbox row in the same transaction as the order and no longer needs a relay that polls the table."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Debezium''s PostgreSQL connector streams changes from the write-ahead log through logical decoding (here with the built-in `pgoutput` plugin), and its outbox event router turns each inserted outbox row into a Kafka record. Only committed transactions reach the log stream, so the guarantee is the same as the relay''s: the event exists exactly when the order change committed. What changes is who moves the rows (a connector instead of a polling loop); the api''s transaction does not change, and consumers still dedupe because delivery is at least once.', 3, 5
