{"compatibilityLevel":"BACKWARD"}

# v1: note is an optional integer, v2: note removed, v3: note is back as an optional string
courier-matcher (v3) partition 4 offset 18233 written with schema v1:
  validation failed: /note must be string
```

Statement: "BACKWARD checked v3 only against v2, which has no `note`; with v1 events still in the topic, BACKWARD_TRANSITIVE (v3 against every earlier version) is the mode that would have refused v3."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The non-transitive modes compare a new schema with the latest registered version only. v2 has no `note`, so v3 passed, although v1 events with an integer `note` are still inside the 7-day retention window. The *_TRANSITIVE modes compare with every earlier version and would have reported the type change against v1. Use a transitive mode whenever old events can still be read: long retention, compacted topics or replays. The misconception is that each check also covers the versions before the last one.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_kfk_171') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', true),
        ('False', false)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);

-- challenge chal_coding_kfk_172 (fix_bug)
INSERT INTO challenge.challenges (public_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, name, title, description, status, difficulty, type, points, is_published, is_deleted)
VALUES ('chal_coding_kfk_172', (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'kafka-event-streaming'), (SELECT id FROM course.module WHERE course_id = (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'kafka-event-streaming') AND order_index = 3 AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), (SELECT id FROM course.lesson WHERE content_source = 'lesson-kfk-3-2' AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), 'schema-compatibility-check', 'Fix the compatibility check', 'Rice Rocket''s CI runs `checkCompatibility(history, candidate, mode)` before a new OrderPlaced schema is registered, and it let the rename of `deliveryAddress` and the newly required `tip` through. The comment at the top of the file states the rules and the exact problem strings. The editor holds the fixed checker with its key blocks missing, each marked by a `// [ ]` line; some pieces are last month''s buggy versions. Fill every line so that: BACKWARD makes the candidate read data written with the earlier version, FORWARD makes the earlier version read data written with the candidate, FULL checks both; the *_TRANSITIVE modes compare with every earlier version, the others only with the latest; a field the reader requires is a problem unless the writer requires it too, default or not; and a field both schemas declare keeps its type. Run feeds schema histories and prints `{ compatible, problems }`.', 'open', 'medium'::difficulty_question, 'fix_bug', 30, true, false)
ON CONFLICT (public_id) DO UPDATE SET
    course_id = EXCLUDED.course_id, module_id = EXCLUDED.module_id, lesson_id = EXCLUDED.lesson_id, name = EXCLUDED.name,
    title = EXCLUDED.title, description = EXCLUDED.description, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, type = EXCLUDED.type,
    points = EXCLUDED.points, is_published = EXCLUDED.is_published, is_deleted = false, updated_at = now();
